{"type":"message","id":"1978022d","parentId":"86633d5a","timestamp":"2026-02-01T16:01:07.306Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Create a comprehensive, beautifully written guide for setting up Clawdbot on AWS EC2 from scratch. This is for Doug's friend who wants to replicate a similar setup. The guide should showcase how powerful Clawdbot is while being practical and followable.\n\nWrite to: /home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/clawdbot-setup-guide/GUIDE.md\n\n## What to cover (in order):\n\n### Part 1: What You're Building\n- Brief intro: what Clawdbot is, what it can do\n- Real examples from our setup (don't include secrets, but show capabilities):\n  - Connected to Telegram + WhatsApp\n  - Runs 15+ background processes (PM2)\n  - Paper trades on Polymarket with 6 strategies\n  - Built a WhatsApp bot for biomedical equipment troubleshooting\n  - Has specialized sub-agents (Blueprint for construction, Ticker for markets)\n  - Does web research, browser automation, file management\n  - Proactive heartbeats, cron jobs, daily standups\n  - Memory system with daily notes + long-term memory\n- Set expectations: \"By the end of this guide, you'll have an AI assistant that knows you, works for you 24/7, and gets smarter over time.\"\n\n### Part 2: AWS EC2 Setup\n- Step by step:\n  - Create AWS account (if needed)\n  - Launch EC2 instance: recommend t3.medium or t3.large, Amazon Linux 2023\n  - Security group: only allow SSH (port 22), restrict to your IP\n  - Key pair creation and SSH access\n  - Elastic IP (so your address doesn't change on restart)\n  - Storage: recommend 30GB+ gp3\n- Post-launch:\n  - SSH in\n  - Update system: sudo dnf update -y\n  - Install Node.js 22: use nvm\n  - Install essential tools: git, pm2, cloudflared, jq\n\n### Part 3: Install Clawdbot\n- npm install -g clawdbot\n- clawdbot init (walkthrough)\n- Directory structure explanation\n- Key files: AGENTS.md, SOUL.md, USER.md, MEMORY.md, IDENTITY.md\n\n### Part 4: API Keys (The Fuel)\nFor each, explain what it does, where to get it, free vs paid, and how to add to config:\n1. **Anthropic (Claude)** — primary brain. Best model: Claude Sonnet 4 (balance of speed/cost/quality) or Opus 4.5 (max quality). Get at console.anthropic.com. Cost: ~$3-15/day depending on usage.\n2. **OpenAI** — for embeddings (memory search). Get at platform.openai.com. Cost: pennies/day.\n3. **Brave Search** — web search. Get at brave.com/search/api. Free tier: 2000 queries/month.\n4. **Telegram Bot** — primary chat interface. Create via @BotFather on Telegram. Free.\n5. **WhatsApp (optional)** — via Meta Business API. Explain the setup briefly.\n6. **Discord (optional)** — bot token from discord.com/developers.\n\n### Part 5: Configuration Deep Dive\n- Show a complete example gateway config (sanitized)\n- Explain each section:\n  - providers (AI models)\n  - channels (Telegram, etc.)\n  - agents (main + sub-agents)\n  - tools (what the AI can access)\n  - security settings\n- How to apply config: clawdbot gateway config.apply or config.patch\n\n### Part 6: Security Hardening (Critical)\n- **DM Policy**: Lock to your user ID only\n- **File permissions**: chmod 700 on workspace, 600 on config\n- **API key safety**: never commit to git, use .env or config\n- **Tool restrictions**: what tools to enable/disable\n- **Exec security**: sandboxing, allowlists\n- **Log redaction**: enable to prevent API keys in logs\n- **Firewall**: only SSH open, everything else via tunnels\n- **Auto-updates**: keep Clawdbot and Node.js current\n- **Backup strategy**: periodic workspace backup\n\n### Part 7: Making It Yours (SOUL.md & Personality)\n- How to write a great SOUL.md\n- Examples of personality traits that work\n- The difference between SOUL.md (who), AGENTS.md (how), USER.md (for whom)\n- Tips: be specific, give opinions, set boundaries\n\n### Part 8: Memory System\n- Daily notes (memory/YYYY-MM-DD.md)\n- Long-term memory (MEMORY.md)\n- Working memory (memory/WORKING.md)\n- How the AI uses these to maintain continuity\n- The golden rule: \"If you want to remember it, write it to a file\"\n\n### Part 9: Cron Jobs & Heartbeats\n- What heartbeats are and why they matter\n- HEARTBEAT.md — the checklist\n- How to set up cron jobs for:\n  - Morning briefings\n  - Periodic checks\n  - Reminders\n- Staggering heartbeats for multi-agent setups\n- Cost management: don't over-heartbeat\n\n### Part 10: Multi-Agent Architecture\n- Why multiple agents > one generalist\n- How to create specialized agents\n- Session keys and isolation\n- Communication between agents (sessions_send, shared files)\n- Practical example: 2-3 agent setup\n  - Main agent (coordinator)\n  - Specialist 1 (domain-specific)\n  - Specialist 2 (monitoring/reporting)\n- Daily standup system\n\n### Part 11: Advanced Features\n- Sub-agent spawning for long tasks\n- Browser automation\n- PM2 process management\n- Cloudflare tunnels for exposing services\n- Building tools and scripts the AI can use\n- Canvas for presenting UIs\n\n### Part 12: Cost Management\n- Typical monthly costs:\n  - EC2 t3.medium: ~$30/month\n  - Anthropic API: $50-200/month depending on model and usage\n  - OpenAI embeddings: $1-5/month\n  - Total: $80-250/month for a powerful setup\n- Tips to reduce costs:\n  - Use cheaper models for routine tasks\n  - Limit heartbeat frequency\n  - Use isolated sessions for cron (don't bloat main context)\n  - Monitor usage via /status\n\n### Part 13: Troubleshooting\n- Common issues and fixes:\n  - Gateway won't start\n  - Bot not responding on Telegram\n  - API key errors\n  - Memory/context too large\n  - PM2 process crashes\n- Useful commands cheat sheet\n\n### Appendix: Quick Reference\n- All clawdbot commands\n- Useful PM2 commands\n- File structure cheat sheet\n- Config template (copy-paste ready)\n\n## Writing style:\n- Conversational but thorough — like a knowledgeable friend walking you through it\n- Use code blocks for all commands\n- Include expected output where helpful\n- Add tips/warnings in callout style (💡 Tip: ... or ⚠️ Warning: ...)\n- Don't be corporate — be real\n- Assume reader is technical but not a DevOps expert\n- Make it feel exciting, not like a chore\n\n## Also create:\n- A quick-start version at QUICKSTART.md (just the essential commands, no explanation, for experienced users)\n- A sample config at examples/gateway-config.example.json (sanitized, well-commented)\n\n## IMPORTANT:\n- Do NOT include any real API keys, tokens, or passwords\n- Do NOT include Doug's personal info\n- This should be shareable publicly\n- Reference official docs: https://docs.clawd.bot and https://github.com/clawdbot/clawdbot\n- Reference ClawdHub for skills: https://clawdhub.com\n- Reference Discord community: https://discord.com/invite/clawd"}],"timestamp":1769961667305}}
