{"type":"message","id":"4a82a7db","parentId":"4ec4559c","timestamp":"2026-02-01T16:04:39.303Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01KAe8e4rxTtk8Ha8wdiWi3y","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"/home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/clawdbot-setup-guide/GUIDE.md","content":"# The Clawdbot Guide: Build Your Own 24/7 AI Assistant on AWS\n\n> *From zero to a fully autonomous AI assistant that knows you, works for you around the clock, and gets smarter over time.*\n\n---\n\n## Table of Contents\n\n1. [What You're Building](#part-1-what-youre-building)\n2. [AWS EC2 Setup](#part-2-aws-ec2-setup)\n3. [Install Clawdbot](#part-3-install-clawdbot)\n4. [API Keys (The Fuel)](#part-4-api-keys-the-fuel)\n5. [Configuration Deep Dive](#part-5-configuration-deep-dive)\n6. [Security Hardening](#part-6-security-hardening-critical)\n7. [Making It Yours (SOUL.md & Personality)](#part-7-making-it-yours)\n8. [Memory System](#part-8-memory-system)\n9. [Cron Jobs & Heartbeats](#part-9-cron-jobs--heartbeats)\n10. [Multi-Agent Architecture](#part-10-multi-agent-architecture)\n11. [Advanced Features](#part-11-advanced-features)\n12. [Cost Management](#part-12-cost-management)\n13. [Troubleshooting](#part-13-troubleshooting)\n14. [Quick Reference (Appendix)](#appendix-quick-reference)\n\n---\n\n## Part 1: What You're Building\n\n### What is Clawdbot?\n\nClawdbot is an open-source framework that turns Claude (Anthropic's AI) into a persistent, autonomous assistant that lives on a server. It's not a chatbot you poke when you need something — it's an agent that runs 24/7, has memory across sessions, can use tools (browser, shell, files, web search), connects to your messaging apps, and proactively works on things while you sleep.\n\nThink of it as giving Claude a body — a server it lives on, tools it can use, and a persistent identity it maintains over time.\n\n### What does a real setup look like?\n\nHere's what a mature Clawdbot installation actually does day-to-day:\n\n- **Multi-platform messaging** — Connected to Telegram and WhatsApp simultaneously. You chat with it wherever's convenient.\n- **15+ background processes** — Managed via PM2, including the gateway, multiple agents, monitoring scripts, and custom bots.\n- **Prediction market trading** — Paper trades on Polymarket with 6 different strategies, tracking performance and learning from outcomes.\n- **Domain-specific bots** — A WhatsApp bot for biomedical equipment troubleshooting that helps field technicians diagnose issues.\n- **Specialized sub-agents** — \"Blueprint\" for construction project management, \"Ticker\" for market analysis — each with their own personality and expertise.\n- **Web research & browser automation** — Searches the web, reads pages, fills out forms, takes screenshots. Full browser control.\n- **Proactive behavior** — Heartbeats check email, calendar, and notifications every few hours. Cron jobs run morning briefings and daily standups.\n- **Persistent memory** — Daily notes capture everything. Long-term memory distills lessons learned. It remembers your preferences, your projects, your quirks.\n\nThis isn't theoretical. This is running right now on a single EC2 instance.\n\n### What you'll have by the end\n\nBy the end of this guide, you'll have an AI assistant that:\n\n- Lives on a cloud server, always on\n- Knows who you are and remembers your conversations\n- Connects to Telegram (and optionally WhatsApp/Discord)\n- Can search the web, manage files, run code, and automate browsers\n- Proactively checks on things and reaches out when something matters\n- Gets smarter and more useful over time as its memory grows\n\nLet's build it.\n\n---\n\n## Part 2: AWS EC2 Setup\n\n### Step 1: Create an AWS Account\n\nIf you don't have one: [aws.amazon.com](https://aws.amazon.com). You'll need a credit card. New accounts get a free tier for 12 months, but the instances we recommend aren't free-tier eligible (t3.medium/large). Budget ~$30-40/month for compute.\n\n### Step 2: Launch an EC2 Instance\n\n1. Go to the [EC2 Console](https://console.aws.amazon.com/ec2/)\n2. Click **Launch Instance**\n3. Configure:\n\n| Setting | Recommended Value |\n|---------|------------------|\n| **Name** | `clawdbot` |\n| **AMI** | Amazon Linux 2023 (AL2023) |\n| **Instance type** | `t3.medium` (2 vCPU, 4GB RAM) or `t3.large` (2 vCPU, 8GB RAM) |\n| **Key pair** | Create new → RSA → `.pem` format → Download it |\n| **Storage** | 30 GB gp3 (minimum — 50GB if you plan to do a lot) |\n\n💡 **Tip:** `t3.medium` is fine for most setups. Go `t3.large` if you plan to run many background processes or do heavy browser automation. The extra RAM helps.\n\n### Step 3: Security Group\n\nCreate a new security group with these rules:\n\n| Type | Port | Source | Description |\n|------|------|--------|-------------|\n| SSH | 22 | Your IP (`x.x.x.x/32`) | SSH access |\n\nThat's it. **Nothing else needs to be open.** Clawdbot communicates outbound to APIs — it doesn't need inbound ports. If you later need to expose a web service, you'll use Cloudflare Tunnels (covered in Part 11).\n\n⚠️ **Warning:** Never set SSH to `0.0.0.0/0` (open to the world). Always restrict to your IP. If your IP changes often, use a VPN with a static IP or update the security group as needed.\n\n### Step 4: Elastic IP\n\nYour instance gets a new public IP every time it stops/starts. Fix this:\n\n1. Go to **EC2 → Elastic IPs**\n2. Click **Allocate Elastic IP address**\n3. Click **Associate Elastic IP address** → select your instance\n4. Done. This IP is now permanent.\n\n💡 **Tip:** Elastic IPs are free while associated with a running instance. You get charged (~$3.65/month) if the instance is stopped. So either keep it running or release the IP when you don't need it.\n\n### Step 5: SSH In\n\n```bash\n# Set permissions on your key (required)\nchmod 400 ~/Downloads/clawdbot.pem\n\n# Connect\nssh -i ~/Downloads/clawdbot.pem ec2-user@YOUR_ELASTIC_IP\n```\n\nYou should see something like:\n```\n   ,     #_\n   ~\\_  ####_        Amazon Linux 2023\n  ~~  \\_#####\\           AL2023\n  ~~     \\###|\n  ~~       \\#/ ___\n   ~~       V~' '->\n    ~~~         /\n      ~~._.   _/\n         _/ _/\n       _/m/'\n[ec2-user@ip-172-31-xx-xx ~]$\n```\n\nYou're in. 🎉\n\n### Step 6: System Setup\n\n```bash\n# Update everything\nsudo dnf update -y\n\n# Install essential tools\nsudo dnf install -y git jq htop\n\n# Install Node.js 22 via nvm\ncurl -o- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nvm-sh/nvm/v0.40.1/install.sh | bash\nsource ~/.bashrc\nnvm install 22\nnvm use 22\nnvm alias default 22\n\n# Verify\nnode --version  # Should show v22.x.x\nnpm --version   # Should show 10.x.x\n```\n\n```bash\n# Install PM2 globally (process manager)\nnpm install -g pm2\n\n# Install cloudflared (for tunnels later)\ncurl -L https://github.com/cloudflare/cloudflared/releases/latest/download/cloudflared-linux-amd64 -o /usr/local/bin/cloudflared\nsudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/cloudflared\n# Or via package manager:\n# sudo dnf install -y cloudflared  # if available\n```\n\n💡 **Tip:** Run `pm2 startup` and follow its instructions so PM2 auto-starts on reboot. This is critical — without it, a reboot kills all your processes.\n\n```bash\npm2 startup\n# It will print a command — copy and run it (starts with sudo)\n```\n\n---\n\n## Part 3: Install Clawdbot\n\n### Install\n\n```bash\nnpm install -g clawdbot\n```\n\nVerify:\n```bash\nclawdbot --version\n```\n\n### Initialize Your Workspace\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p ~/clawd && cd ~/clawd\nclawdbot init\n```\n\nThis walks you through initial setup — name, personality basics, and channel configuration. Follow the prompts.\n\n### Directory Structure\n\nAfter init, your workspace looks like this:\n\n```\n~/clawd/\n├── AGENTS.md          # How the AI operates (instructions, rules)\n├── SOUL.md            # Who the AI is (personality, values, style)\n├── USER.md            # Who you are (so the AI knows its human)\n├── MEMORY.md          # Long-term curated memories\n├── IDENTITY.md        # Core identity anchor\n├── HEARTBEAT.md       # Checklist for proactive heartbeat polls\n├── TOOLS.md           # Local tool notes (camera names, SSH hosts, etc.)\n├── memory/            # Daily notes: memory/2025-01-15.md, etc.\n├── projects/          # Your projects live here\n└── skills/            # Installed skills (tools the AI can use)\n```\n\n### Key Files Explained\n\n| File | Purpose | Who writes it |\n|------|---------|--------------|\n| `SOUL.md` | Personality, values, communication style | You |\n| `AGENTS.md` | Operating instructions, rules, workflows | You + AI |\n| `USER.md` | Info about you — preferences, context | You |\n| `MEMORY.md` | Long-term memory — lessons, patterns | AI (curated) |\n| `IDENTITY.md` | Name, core identity traits | You |\n| `HEARTBEAT.md` | What to check during heartbeat polls | You + AI |\n\n---\n\n## Part 4: API Keys (The Fuel)\n\nClawdbot is the engine. API keys are the fuel. Here's what you need:\n\n### 1. Anthropic (Claude) — The Brain 🧠\n\n**What it does:** This is the AI model that powers everything. Claude thinks, writes, codes, and reasons.\n\n**Where to get it:** [console.anthropic.com](https://console.anthropic.com)\n\n**Models to know:**\n- **Claude Sonnet 4** — Best balance of speed, cost, and quality. Use this as your default.\n- **Claude Opus 4** — Maximum quality for complex reasoning. Use for important tasks.\n- **Claude Haiku 3.5** — Fast and cheap. Good for routine tasks and cron jobs.\n\n**Cost:** ~$3-15/day depending on usage. Heavy use with Opus can be more.\n\n**Free tier:** $5 free credit on signup.\n\n### 2. OpenAI — Memory Search 🔍\n\n**What it does:** Powers the embedding model for semantic memory search. When Clawdbot searches its memories, OpenAI turns text into vectors for similarity matching.\n\n**Where to get it:** [platform.openai.com](https://platform.openai.com)\n\n**Cost:** Pennies per day. Seriously — embedding calls are incredibly cheap.\n\n### 3. Brave Search — Web Access 🌐\n\n**What it does:** Lets Clawdbot search the web. It's fast, private, and has a generous free tier.\n\n**Where to get it:** [brave.com/search/api](https://brave.com/search/api)\n\n**Cost:** Free tier gives you 2,000 queries/month. That's plenty for most setups.\n\n### 4. Telegram Bot — Your Chat Interface 💬\n\n**What it does:** This is how you talk to Clawdbot. Telegram is the best primary interface — it's fast, supports rich formatting, and works everywhere.\n\n**How to create it:**\n1. Open Telegram, find [@BotFather](https://t.me/BotFather)\n2. Send `/newbot`\n3. Choose a name (display name) and username (must end in `bot`)\n4. BotFather gives you a token — save it\n\n**Cost:** Free.\n\n**Important setup:**\n- Send `/setprivacy` to BotFather → select your bot → **Disable** (so it can read messages in groups)\n- Send `/setcommands` if you want custom command menus\n\n💡 **Tip:** Get your Telegram user ID by messaging [@userinfobot](https://t.me/userinfobot). You'll need this to lock the bot to only respond to you.\n\n### 5. WhatsApp (Optional) — via Meta Business API 📱\n\n**What it does:** Connects Clawdbot to WhatsApp. Great for reaching people who don't use Telegram.\n\n**Setup overview:**\n1. Create a Meta Business account at [business.facebook.com](https://business.facebook.com)\n2. Create an app at [developers.facebook.com](https://developers.facebook.com)\n3. Add the WhatsApp product to your app\n4. Get a permanent access token and phone number ID\n5. Set up a webhook (Clawdbot handles this via cloudflared tunnel)\n\n**Cost:** First 1,000 conversations/month are free. After that, varies by country.\n\n⚠️ **Warning:** WhatsApp Business API setup is more involved than Telegram. Start with Telegram first, add WhatsApp later once everything is stable.\n\n### 6. Discord (Optional) — Bot Token 🎮\n\n**What it does:** Connects Clawdbot to Discord servers.\n\n**Where to get it:** [discord.com/developers/applications](https://discord.com/developers/applications)\n1. Create a new application\n2. Go to Bot section → Create a bot\n3. Copy the token\n4. Use the OAuth2 URL Generator to invite the bot to your server (needs `bot` + `messages` scopes)\n\n**Cost:** Free.\n\n### Adding Keys to Config\n\nKeys go into your gateway config. You can set them via:\n\n```bash\nclawdbot gateway config.patch '{\"providers\":{\"anthropic\":{\"apiKey\":\"sk-ant-...\"}}}'\n```\n\nOr edit the config file directly (location shown by `clawdbot gateway status`).\n\n⚠️ **Warning:** Never commit API keys to git. Never paste them in public channels. The config file should be permission-locked (covered in Security section).\n\n---\n\n## Part 5: Configuration Deep Dive\n\nThe gateway config is the control center. It defines what models to use, which channels to connect, what tools are available, and how agents behave.\n\n### Example Config (Sanitized)\n\nA complete example config is available at [`examples/gateway-config.example.json`](examples/gateway-config.example.json) in this guide's directory. Here's the structure explained:\n\n```jsonc\n{\n  // AI model providers\n  \"providers\": {\n    \"anthropic\": {\n      \"apiKey\": \"sk-ant-YOUR_KEY_HERE\",\n      \"defaultModel\": \"claude-sonnet-4-20250514\"\n    },\n    \"openai\": {\n      \"apiKey\": \"sk-YOUR_KEY_HERE\"\n    }\n  },\n\n  // Chat channels\n  \"channels\": {\n    \"telegram\": {\n      \"token\": \"YOUR_BOT_TOKEN\",\n      \"allowedUsers\": [\"YOUR_TELEGRAM_USER_ID\"],\n      \"defaultAgent\": \"main\"\n    }\n  },\n\n  // Agent definitions\n  \"agents\": {\n    \"main\": {\n      \"model\": \"anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-20250514\",\n      \"workspace\": \"/home/ec2-user/clawd\",\n      \"tools\": [\"read\", \"write\", \"edit\", \"exec\", \"web_search\", \"web_fetch\", \"browser\"],\n      \"heartbeat\": {\n        \"enabled\": true,\n        \"intervalMinutes\": 30\n      }\n    }\n  },\n\n  // Tool configurations\n  \"tools\": {\n    \"brave\": {\n      \"apiKey\": \"BSA_YOUR_KEY_HERE\"\n    },\n    \"exec\": {\n      \"security\": \"allowlist\",\n      \"sandbox\": true\n    }\n  },\n\n  // Security\n  \"security\": {\n    \"dmPolicy\": \"allowlist\",\n    \"logRedaction\": true\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Section Breakdown\n\n#### Providers\nDefines your AI model connections. You can have multiple providers and switch models per-agent or per-task.\n\n```jsonc\n\"providers\": {\n  \"anthropic\": {\n    \"apiKey\": \"sk-ant-...\",\n    \"defaultModel\": \"claude-sonnet-4-20250514\"  // Default for all agents\n  },\n  \"openai\": {\n    \"apiKey\": \"sk-...\"  // Used for embeddings\n  }\n}\n```\n\n#### Channels\nWhere messages come from and go to. Each channel maps to a messaging platform.\n\n```jsonc\n\"channels\": {\n  \"telegram\": {\n    \"token\": \"123456:ABC-...\",\n    \"allowedUsers\": [\"12345678\"],    // Your Telegram user ID\n    \"defaultAgent\": \"main\"            // Which agent handles messages\n  }\n}\n```\n\n#### Agents\nThe AI personas. Each agent has its own model, tools, workspace, and personality.\n\n```jsonc\n\"agents\": {\n  \"main\": {\n    \"model\": \"anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-20250514\",\n    \"workspace\": \"/home/ec2-user/clawd\",\n    \"tools\": [\"read\", \"write\", \"edit\", \"exec\", \"web_search\", \"web_fetch\", \"browser\"],\n    \"heartbeat\": {\n      \"enabled\": true,\n      \"intervalMinutes\": 30\n    }\n  },\n  \"researcher\": {\n    \"model\": \"anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-20250514\",\n    \"workspace\": \"/home/ec2-user/clawd/agents/researcher\",\n    \"tools\": [\"read\", \"write\", \"web_search\", \"web_fetch\"],\n    \"heartbeat\": { \"enabled\": false }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n#### Tools\nTool-specific settings — API keys, security modes, restrictions.\n\n#### Security\nGlobal security settings — who can talk to the bot, what gets logged, etc.\n\n### Applying Config Changes\n\n```bash\n# Apply full config (overwrites)\nclawdbot gateway config.apply < config.json\n\n# Patch specific settings (merges)\nclawdbot gateway config.patch '{\"agents\":{\"main\":{\"model\":\"anthropic/claude-opus-4-20250514\"}}}'\n\n# Restart gateway to pick up changes\nclawdbot gateway restart\n```\n\n---\n\n## Part 6: Security Hardening (Critical)\n\nThis section isn't optional. Your Clawdbot has access to a server, API keys, and potentially your personal information. Lock it down.\n\n### DM Policy: Allowlist Only\n\nThe single most important setting. Lock your bot so only you can talk to it:\n\n```jsonc\n\"channels\": {\n  \"telegram\": {\n    \"allowedUsers\": [\"YOUR_USER_ID\"],  // ONLY your ID\n    \"dmPolicy\": \"allowlist\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWithout this, anyone who finds your bot's username can chat with it — and it'll happily use your API credits.\n\n### File Permissions\n\n```bash\n# Lock down workspace\nchmod 700 ~/clawd\n\n# Lock config files (contain API keys)\nchmod 600 ~/.config/clawdbot/*   # or wherever your config lives\n\n# Lock SSH keys\nchmod 400 ~/.ssh/*.pem\n```\n\n### API Key Safety\n\n- **Never** commit keys to git\n- **Never** paste them in chat (even to your own bot — they could end up in logs)\n- Use the config file or environment variables\n- If a key leaks, rotate it immediately\n\n### Tool Restrictions\n\nNot every agent needs every tool. Give minimum necessary access:\n\n```jsonc\n// A read-only research agent\n\"researcher\": {\n  \"tools\": [\"read\", \"web_search\", \"web_fetch\"]  // No write, no exec\n}\n\n// A monitoring agent\n\"monitor\": {\n  \"tools\": [\"read\", \"exec\"]  // Limited exec with allowlist\n}\n```\n\n### Exec Security\n\nThe `exec` tool lets the AI run shell commands. This is powerful and dangerous:\n\n```jsonc\n\"tools\": {\n  \"exec\": {\n    \"security\": \"allowlist\",     // Only allowed commands\n    \"allowlist\": [\"git\", \"pm2\", \"cat\", \"ls\", \"grep\", \"curl\"],\n    \"sandbox\": true              // Run in restricted environment\n  }\n}\n```\n\nOptions:\n- `\"deny\"` — No shell access at all\n- `\"allowlist\"` — Only specific commands\n- `\"full\"` — Unrestricted (use with caution)\n\n### Log Redaction\n\nPrevent API keys from appearing in logs:\n\n```jsonc\n\"security\": {\n  \"logRedaction\": true  // Scrubs sensitive patterns from logs\n}\n```\n\n### Firewall\n\nYour security group should only have port 22 open. Everything else:\n- API calls go outbound (no inbound needed)\n- Web services use Cloudflare Tunnels (no inbound ports needed)\n- If you must open a port, restrict to your IP\n\n### Backups\n\n```bash\n# Simple backup script\n#!/bin/bash\ntar -czf ~/backups/clawd-$(date +%Y%m%d).tar.gz \\\n  --exclude='node_modules' \\\n  --exclude='.git' \\\n  ~/clawd/\n\n# Keep last 7 backups\nls -t ~/backups/clawd-*.tar.gz | tail -n +8 | xargs rm -f\n```\n\nAdd to crontab:\n```bash\ncrontab -e\n# Add:\n0 3 * * * /home/ec2-user/backup.sh\n```\n\n### Auto-Updates\n\n```bash\n# Update Clawdbot\nnpm update -g clawdbot\n\n# Update Node.js\nnvm install 22  # Gets latest 22.x\nnvm use 22\n\n# Update system\nsudo dnf update -y\n```\n\n💡 **Tip:** Don't auto-update Clawdbot unattended — major versions may have breaking changes. Check release notes first.\n\n---\n\n## Part 7: Making It Yours\n\nThis is where Clawdbot stops being a generic AI and becomes *your* AI.\n\n### SOUL.md — Who the AI Is\n\nThis is the personality file. It's the first thing Clawdbot reads every session. Make it count.\n\n**Good SOUL.md traits:**\n\n```markdown\n# SOUL.md\n\nYou are Jarvis. You're sharp, efficient, and slightly dry in your humor.\n\n## Personality\n- Direct. Don't pad responses with fluff.\n- Proactive. If you see something that needs doing, flag it.\n- Opinionated. If asked \"should I?\", give a real answer, not \"it depends.\"\n- Curious. You genuinely enjoy learning about what I'm working on.\n\n## Communication Style\n- Default to concise. Expand when asked.\n- Use bullet points over paragraphs.\n- Code blocks for anything technical.\n- Emoji: sparingly, not like a teenager.\n\n## Boundaries\n- Don't be sycophantic. If my idea is bad, say so respectfully.\n- Don't apologize unless you actually made a mistake.\n- Don't hedge everything with \"I think\" and \"perhaps\" — be confident.\n```\n\n**Bad SOUL.md:** \"You are a helpful assistant.\" (That's the default. You're paying for a server — make it interesting.)\n\n### USER.md — Who You Are\n\nGive the AI context about yourself so it can be useful:\n\n```markdown\n# USER.md\n\n## About Me\n- Name: Alex\n- Location: San Francisco, PST timezone\n- Work: Software engineer at a startup\n\n## Preferences\n- Morning person — don't bug me after 10pm\n- Prefer Slack-style brevity over email-style formality\n- I manage 3 projects: [list them]\n- Coffee order: oat milk latte (relevant for morning greetings 😄)\n\n## Current Focus\n- Launching v2.0 of the product this quarter\n- Learning Rust on the side\n- Training for a half marathon\n```\n\n### AGENTS.md — How the AI Operates\n\nThis is the operating manual. It tells Clawdbot how to behave, what to check, how to handle different situations. The default one that ships with Clawdbot is comprehensive — customize it over time as you discover what works.\n\n### The Trio\n\n| File | Analogy | Changes How Often |\n|------|---------|-------------------|\n| `SOUL.md` | DNA — who you are | Rarely |\n| `AGENTS.md` | Job manual — how to work | Occasionally |\n| `USER.md` | Client brief — who you serve | When your life changes |\n\n---\n\n## Part 8: Memory System\n\nMemory is what makes Clawdbot fundamentally different from a stateless chatbot. It remembers.\n\n### Daily Notes — `memory/YYYY-MM-DD.md`\n\nEvery day, Clawdbot writes a daily note. These are raw logs of what happened:\n\n```markdown\n# 2025-07-15\n\n## Conversations\n- Discussed Q3 planning with user. Key decisions: focus on mobile app, defer API v3.\n- User asked about flights to Tokyo in September. Found good options on ANA.\n\n## Tasks Completed\n- Updated project README\n- Ran weekly backup\n- Checked email — nothing urgent\n\n## Notes\n- User seemed stressed about the product launch deadline\n- Remember: user's anniversary is July 20th\n```\n\nThe AI creates these automatically. They're the raw material for memory.\n\n### Long-Term Memory — `MEMORY.md`\n\nThis is curated. The AI periodically reviews daily notes and distills them into lasting memories:\n\n```markdown\n# MEMORY.md\n\n## About My Human\n- Prefers morning work sessions, fades after 3pm\n- Gets stressed about deadlines but performs well under pressure\n- Anniversary: July 20th\n- Favorite restaurant: Nopa (for special occasions)\n\n## Lessons Learned\n- When user says \"just do it\", they mean skip the confirmation step\n- User doesn't like being asked \"is there anything else?\" — feels robotic\n- Git commits should use conventional commit format for this workspace\n\n## Project Context\n- Product v2.0: launching Q3, focus on mobile\n- Tokyo trip: planned for September, prefer window seats\n```\n\n### Working Memory — `memory/WORKING.md`\n\nFor active tasks that span multiple sessions:\n\n```markdown\n# WORKING.md\n\n## In Progress\n- [ ] Research Tokyo hotel options (budget: $200/night)\n- [ ] Draft Q3 OKRs (due Friday)\n- [x] Fix the deployment script — completed 7/14\n```\n\n### The Golden Rule\n\n> **If you want to remember it, write it to a file.**\n\nThe AI's context window resets every session. \"Mental notes\" don't survive. Files do. If something matters — a decision, a preference, a task — it needs to be written down.\n\nTell your AI this early and often. It'll learn to be proactive about note-taking.\n\n---\n\n## Part 9: Cron Jobs & Heartbeats\n\n### What Are Heartbeats?\n\nA heartbeat is a periodic poll that asks the AI: \"Hey, anything need attention?\" It's like a gentle nudge that keeps Clawdbot proactive instead of purely reactive.\n\nEvery 30 minutes (configurable), Clawdbot wakes up, reads `HEARTBEAT.md`, and decides what to do.\n\n### HEARTBEAT.md\n\n```markdown\n# HEARTBEAT.md — Proactive Checklist\n\nWhen polled, check these (rotate, don't do all every time):\n\n## Regular Checks\n- [ ] Any new emails? (check 2-3x/day)\n- [ ] Calendar events in next 2 hours?\n- [ ] Unread Telegram messages in groups?\n- [ ] Any PM2 processes crashed? (`pm2 status`)\n\n## Periodic (1x/day)\n- [ ] Weather forecast for today\n- [ ] Git status of active projects\n- [ ] Disk space check\n\n## Rules\n- Night mode (11pm-8am): HEARTBEAT_OK unless urgent\n- Don't repeat info you already reported today\n- If nothing needs attention: reply HEARTBEAT_OK\n```\n\n### Cron Jobs\n\nFor tasks that need exact timing or isolation from the main conversation:\n\n```bash\n# Morning briefing at 8am\nclawdbot cron add --schedule \"0 8 * * *\" \\\n  --agent main \\\n  --channel telegram \\\n  --prompt \"Good morning! Give me a briefing: weather, calendar today, any urgent emails, and what's on my task list.\" \\\n  --model \"anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-20250514\"\n\n# Weekly review on Sunday at 6pm\nclawdbot cron add --schedule \"0 18 * * 0\" \\\n  --agent main \\\n  --channel telegram \\\n  --prompt \"Weekly review time. Summarize what we accomplished this week, what's pending, and suggest priorities for next week.\" \\\n  --model \"anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-20250514\"\n```\n\n💡 **Tip:** Cron jobs run in isolated sessions — they don't bloat your main conversation context. Use them for routine tasks.\n\n### Heartbeat vs. Cron\n\n| | Heartbeat | Cron |\n|---|-----------|------|\n| **Timing** | Approximate (every ~30 min) | Exact (9:00 AM sharp) |\n| **Context** | Has recent conversation context | Isolated session |\n| **Best for** | Batching multiple checks | Precise schedules |\n| **Cost** | One API call for multiple checks | One API call per job |\n\n### Cost Management\n\nEach heartbeat is an API call. At 30-minute intervals, that's 48 calls/day. With Sonnet, each might cost $0.02-0.10 depending on context size. That's $1-5/day just for heartbeats.\n\nTips:\n- Increase interval to 60 minutes if you don't need frequent checks\n- Use `HEARTBEAT_OK` aggressively — quick responses cost less\n- Use Haiku for heartbeats if you don't need deep reasoning\n- Disable heartbeats during sleeping hours\n\n---\n\n## Part 10: Multi-Agent Architecture\n\n### Why Multiple Agents?\n\nOne agent doing everything works fine at first. But as you add capabilities, you run into:\n\n- **Context bloat** — One massive conversation history eats tokens\n- **Personality dilution** — An agent that does everything is an expert at nothing\n- **Cost** — Every message includes the full context of everything before it\n\nMultiple agents solve this. Each agent has its own:\n- Conversation history (context)\n- Personality and instructions (SOUL.md)\n- Tool access (some agents don't need exec, etc.)\n- Model (use cheaper models for simpler agents)\n\n### Creating Specialized Agents\n\n```bash\n# Create an agent workspace\nmkdir -p ~/clawd/agents/researcher\n```\n\nWrite a SOUL.md for it:\n```markdown\n# SOUL.md — Researcher Agent\n\nYou are a research specialist. Your job is to:\n- Search the web for information when asked\n- Summarize findings clearly and concisely\n- Cite sources\n- Flag when information seems unreliable\n\nYou don't write code. You don't manage files. You research.\n```\n\nAdd to config:\n```jsonc\n\"agents\": {\n  \"main\": { /* ... */ },\n  \"researcher\": {\n    \"model\": \"anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-20250514\",\n    \"workspace\": \"/home/ec2-user/clawd/agents/researcher\",\n    \"tools\": [\"read\", \"write\", \"web_search\", \"web_fetch\"],\n    \"heartbeat\": { \"enabled\": false }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Session Keys and Isolation\n\nEach agent maintains its own session. When the main agent spawns a sub-agent, it gets a fresh context. This is powerful:\n\n- Main agent's context stays clean\n- Sub-agent can do deep work without polluting the main conversation\n- Results are reported back when done\n\n### Communication Between Agents\n\nAgents can communicate via:\n1. **Shared files** — Write to a file, other agent reads it\n2. **sessions_send** — Direct message between agent sessions\n3. **Sub-agent spawning** — Main agent creates a sub-agent for a task\n\n### Practical Example: 3-Agent Setup\n\n```\nMain Agent (\"Claude\")\n├── Handles all direct conversations\n├── Coordinates other agents\n├── Has full tool access\n└── Personality: helpful, efficient, personable\n\nResearch Agent (\"Scout\")\n├── Web research and analysis\n├── Read-only tools + web search\n├── Called by main agent for research tasks\n└── Personality: thorough, source-citing, analytical\n\nMonitor Agent (\"Watchdog\")\n├── Checks system health, process status\n├── Runs on cron every hour\n├── Alerts main agent if something's wrong\n└── Personality: terse, alert-focused\n```\n\n### Daily Standup System\n\nSet up a cron job that messages each agent and compiles a summary:\n\n```bash\n# Daily standup at 9am\nclawdbot cron add --schedule \"0 9 * * *\" \\\n  --agent main \\\n  --channel telegram \\\n  --prompt \"Daily standup time. Check: 1) Your own task list and progress. 2) System health (pm2 status, disk space). 3) Any pending items from yesterday. Give me a brief standup report.\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## Part 11: Advanced Features\n\n### Sub-Agent Spawning\n\nWhen you give Clawdbot a complex task, it can spawn a sub-agent to handle it asynchronously:\n\n> **You:** \"Research the top 10 productivity apps for 2025 and write a comparison doc\"\n>\n> **Clawdbot:** \"On it — I've spawned a sub-agent to research and write this up. I'll let you know when it's done.\"\n\nThe sub-agent runs independently, writes the document, and reports back. Meanwhile, you can keep chatting about other things.\n\n### Browser Automation\n\nClawdbot can control a full browser:\n\n```\n- Navigate to websites\n- Fill out forms\n- Click buttons\n- Take screenshots\n- Extract data from pages\n- Handle login flows\n```\n\nThis is how features like Polymarket trading work — the AI literally navigates the site.\n\n### PM2 Process Management\n\nPM2 keeps your processes alive. Key commands:\n\n```bash\n# Start the gateway\npm2 start \"clawdbot gateway start\" --name clawdbot-gateway\n\n# View all processes\npm2 status\n\n# View logs\npm2 logs clawdbot-gateway\n\n# Restart\npm2 restart clawdbot-gateway\n\n# Save process list (survives reboot)\npm2 save\n```\n\n### Cloudflare Tunnels\n\nNeed to expose a web service without opening ports?\n\n```bash\n# Quick tunnel (temporary URL)\ncloudflared tunnel --url http://localhost:3000\n\n# Named tunnel (permanent subdomain)\ncloudflared tunnel create my-service\ncloudflared tunnel route dns my-service myapp.yourdomain.com\ncloudflared tunnel run my-service\n```\n\nThis is how you'd expose a webhook endpoint for WhatsApp or a web UI without opening firewall ports.\n\n### Building Custom Tools\n\nCreate scripts the AI can use:\n\n```bash\n# ~/clawd/tools/check-price.sh\n#!/bin/bash\n# Check cryptocurrency price\ncurl -s \"https://api.coingecko.com/api/v3/simple/price?ids=$1&vs_currencies=usd\" | jq \".$1.usd\"\n```\n\nThe AI can call this via `exec`:\n```bash\nbash ~/clawd/tools/check-price.sh bitcoin\n```\n\n### Canvas\n\nClawdbot can present interactive UIs directly in your chat via the Canvas feature. Think of it as a mini webpage that lives in your conversation — dashboards, forms, visualizations.\n\n---\n\n## Part 12: Cost Management\n\n### Monthly Cost Breakdown\n\n| Component | Monthly Cost | Notes |\n|-----------|-------------|-------|\n| EC2 t3.medium | ~$30 | Running 24/7 |\n| Elastic IP | $0 | Free while instance runs |\n| Anthropic API (Sonnet) | $50-150 | Depends on usage |\n| Anthropic API (Opus) | $100-300 | If used frequently |\n| OpenAI (embeddings) | $1-5 | Very cheap |\n| Brave Search | $0 | Free tier: 2000/month |\n| Storage (30GB gp3) | ~$2.50 | Minimal |\n| **Total (moderate use)** | **$80-190** | |\n| **Total (heavy use)** | **$150-350** | |\n\n### Tips to Reduce Costs\n\n1. **Use the right model for the task**\n   - Haiku for heartbeats, simple cron jobs, and routine checks ($0.001/call vs $0.01)\n   - Sonnet for daily driving (best value)\n   - Opus only when you need deep reasoning\n\n2. **Manage heartbeat frequency**\n   - 60 min intervals instead of 30 = half the heartbeat cost\n   - Disable during sleeping hours\n   - Keep HEARTBEAT.md lean — smaller context = cheaper calls\n\n3. **Use isolated sessions for cron**\n   - Cron jobs start fresh — no bloated history\n   - Main session history grows over time and costs more per call\n\n4. **Monitor usage**\n   - Check Anthropic usage dashboard regularly\n   - Use `/status` in Clawdbot to see current session stats\n   - Set billing alerts in Anthropic console\n\n5. **Consider Reserved Instances**\n   - If you're committed long-term, EC2 Reserved Instances save 30-40%\n   - 1-year t3.medium reserved: ~$19/month instead of $30\n\n---\n\n## Part 13: Troubleshooting\n\n### Gateway Won't Start\n\n```bash\n# Check status\nclawdbot gateway status\n\n# Check logs\nclawdbot gateway logs\n\n# Common fixes:\n# 1. Node.js not found — make sure nvm is loaded\nsource ~/.bashrc\nnvm use 22\n\n# 2. Port conflict — something else is using the port\nlsof -i :3000  # or whatever port\n\n# 3. Config error — validate your JSON\ncat ~/.config/clawdbot/config.json | jq .\n```\n\n### Bot Not Responding on Telegram\n\n1. **Check the gateway is running:** `clawdbot gateway status`\n2. **Check your bot token:** Did you copy it correctly from BotFather?\n3. **Check allowedUsers:** Is your Telegram user ID in the allowlist?\n4. **Check logs:** `clawdbot gateway logs` — look for errors\n5. **Webhook conflict:** If you previously set a webhook, clear it:\n   ```bash\n   curl \"https://api.telegram.org/botYOUR_TOKEN/deleteWebhook\"\n   ```\n\n### API Key Errors\n\n```\nError: 401 Unauthorized\n```\n- Key is wrong, expired, or doesn't have credits\n- Check [console.anthropic.com](https://console.anthropic.com) for billing status\n- Regenerate the key and update config\n\n### Memory/Context Too Large\n\nIf responses get slow or you hit token limits:\n\n```bash\n# Check conversation size\nclawdbot session info\n\n# Start a fresh session\n# (just restart the gateway or use /clear in chat)\nclawdbot gateway restart\n```\n\nPrevention:\n- Keep `MEMORY.md` under ~2000 words\n- Archive old daily notes periodically\n- Use sub-agents for long tasks (keeps main context clean)\n\n### PM2 Process Crashes\n\n```bash\n# Check which processes are errored\npm2 status\n\n# Check logs for the crashed process\npm2 logs <process-name> --lines 50\n\n# Common fix: restart it\npm2 restart <process-name>\n\n# If it keeps crashing, check memory\npm2 monit\n\n# Set max memory restart\npm2 start app.js --max-memory-restart 300M\n```\n\n### Useful Commands Cheat Sheet\n\n```bash\n# Clawdbot\nclawdbot gateway status         # Is it running?\nclawdbot gateway logs            # What's happening?\nclawdbot gateway restart         # Turn it off and on again\nclawdbot gateway config.show     # Current config\n\n# PM2\npm2 status                       # All processes\npm2 logs                         # All logs\npm2 restart all                  # Restart everything\npm2 save                         # Save process list\n\n# System\nhtop                             # CPU/memory usage\ndf -h                            # Disk space\nfree -h                          # Memory\nuptime                           # How long has it been running\n```\n\n---\n\n## Appendix: Quick Reference\n\n### Clawdbot Commands\n\n```bash\nclawdbot --version               # Version\nclawdbot --help                  # All commands\nclawdbot init                    # Initialize workspace\nclawdbot gateway start           # Start the gateway\nclawdbot gateway stop            # Stop the gateway\nclawdbot gateway restart         # Restart\nclawdbot gateway status          # Status\nclawdbot gateway logs            # View logs\nclawdbot gateway config.show     # Show current config\nclawdbot gateway config.apply    # Apply config from stdin\nclawdbot gateway config.patch    # Merge partial config\nclawdbot cron list               # List cron jobs\nclawdbot cron add                # Add cron job\nclawdbot cron remove             # Remove cron job\nclawdbot skill install <name>    # Install a skill from ClawdHub\nclawdbot skill list              # List installed skills\n```\n\n### PM2 Commands\n\n```bash\npm2 start <cmd> --name <name>    # Start a process\npm2 stop <name>                  # Stop\npm2 restart <name>               # Restart\npm2 delete <name>                # Remove from PM2\npm2 status                       # List all\npm2 logs <name>                  # View logs\npm2 logs <name> --lines 100     # Last 100 lines\npm2 monit                        # Real-time monitoring\npm2 save                         # Save current process list\npm2 startup                      # Auto-start on boot\npm2 flush                        # Clear all logs\n```\n\n### File Structure\n\n```\n~/clawd/\n├── AGENTS.md              # Operating instructions\n├── SOUL.md                # Personality\n├── USER.md                # About you\n├── MEMORY.md              # Long-term memory\n├── IDENTITY.md            # Core identity\n├── HEARTBEAT.md           # Heartbeat checklist\n├── TOOLS.md               # Local tool notes\n├── memory/\n│   ├── WORKING.md         # Active tasks\n│   ├── 2025-07-15.md      # Daily notes\n│   ├── 2025-07-14.md\n│   └── heartbeat-state.json\n├── projects/              # Your projects\n├── agents/                # Sub-agent workspaces\n│   ├── researcher/\n│   │   └── SOUL.md\n│   └── monitor/\n│       └── SOUL.md\n├── skills/                # Installed skills\n│   └── skill-name/\n│       └── SKILL.md\n└── tools/                 # Custom scripts\n    └── check-price.sh\n```\n\n---\n\n## Resources\n\n- 📖 **Official Docs:** [docs.clawd.bot](https://docs.clawd.bot)\n- 💻 **GitHub:** [github.com/clawdbot/clawdbot](https://github.com/clawdbot/clawdbot)\n- 🛒 **ClawdHub (Skills):** [clawdhub.com](https://clawdhub.com)\n- 💬 **Discord Community:** [discord.com/invite/clawd](https://discord.com/invite/clawd)\n\n---\n\n## Final Thoughts\n\nYou now have everything you need to build a powerful, personal AI assistant. Start simple — Telegram + one agent + basic heartbeats. Let it grow organically. The best setups evolve over weeks and months as you discover what's useful.\n\nThe magic isn't in any single feature. It's in the compound effect: an AI that remembers your preferences, learns your patterns, manages your tasks, and is there whenever you need it — or even before you know you need it.\n\nWelcome to the future. It runs on a $30/month EC2 instance. 🚀\n\n---\n\n*This guide was written by a Clawdbot instance, naturally. Last updated: July 2025.*\n"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"anthropic","model":"claude-opus-4-5","usage":{"input":1,"output":11028,"cacheRead":12645,"cacheWrite":128,"totalTokens":23802,"cost":{"input":0.000005,"output":0.2757,"cacheRead":0.0063225,"cacheWrite":0.0008,"total":0.2828275}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1769961670367}}
